INT. VFW HALL KITCHEN - NIGHT

A wake in the next room: muffled laughter, clinking glasses. Here, under buzzing fluorescents, a steel counter holds trays of cold cuts, a coffee urn, and a single untouched casserole with a foil lid.

ARLO PENDERGAST, 60s, in a dark suit gone shiny at the elbows, stands at the sink scrubbing a pot far past clean. His son's funeral program is folded in his breast pocket.

BILLIE STOKES, 50s, his sister, in a black dress and sneakers, leans against the fridge with a plastic cup of wine. Her eyes are swollen but dry.

CEDRIC NAKAMURA, 30s, Arlo's nephew, comes through the swinging door with a tray of empties. Loosened tie. Fresh shaving cut.

CEDRIC
They're asking for a speech.

ARLO
Who is?

CEDRIC
Everybody.

BILLIE
Tell them he's busy.

CEDRIC
Doing what? Dishes?

BILLIE
Don't.

ARLO
(without turning)
Somebody has to do them.

CEDRIC
Uncle Arlo, it's Danny's wake.

ARLO
I know whose wake it is.

CEDRIC
Then come out. Please.

ARLO
After the pot.

BILLIE
It's been clean for ten minutes.

ARLO
It's not clean.

BILLIE
Arlo.

ARLO
It is not clean.

He scrubs harder. The sponge squeals. Cedric sets the tray on the counter and glances at Billie, who gives a tiny shake of her head.

CEDRIC
(low)
He's been like this since the service.

BILLIE
He's been like this since March.

ARLO
I can hear you.

BILLIE
Good.

The swinging door opens. FATHER ANSELM, 70s, enters in his black clerical collar, a dinner roll in one hand and a napkin in the other. Round-faced, kind, tired.

FATHER ANSELM
There you are, Arlo. I wondered where you'd gone.

ARLO
Washing.

FATHER ANSELM
So I see.

BILLIE
Father, he doesn't want to talk.

FATHER ANSELM
That's quite all right. I'll talk. Cedric, would you fetch more ice?

CEDRIC
Sure.

He goes. The door flaps shut. Anselm eases onto a stool, sets the roll on the counter, and folds his hands.

FATHER ANSELM (CONT'D)
Danny was a good boy.

ARLO
Was he?

FATHER ANSELM
Arlo.

ARLO
He was a good boy until he wasn't.

BILLIE
Don't do this here.

ARLO
Where, then? In front of the whole town?

BILLIE
Not now.

ARLO
He called me from the highway. Did you know that?

BILLIE
(quietly)
No.

ARLO
He called me from the highway at two in the morning and said "Dad, I'm sorry." And I said, "For what?" And he said "For all of it." And I said "Go home and sleep."

FATHER ANSELM
You couldn't have known.

ARLO
That's what you all keep saying.

BILLIE
Because it's true.

ARLO
It's not true. I knew. I knew and I said go home and sleep.

The pot clatters into the sink. Billie crosses the kitchen, takes his wet hands in hers, and holds them against her black dress.

BILLIE
Arlo. Look at me.

ARLO
I can't.

BILLIE
Look at me.

He does. His face crumples like paper in a fist. He doesn't make a sound. Anselm lowers his gaze to the roll. The wake rumbles on through the wall, a hundred voices pretending.

The kitchen door swings open again. Cedric stands with an ice bucket in both hands, pale.

CEDRIC
Uncle Arlo. There's someone here.

ARLO
Who?

CEDRIC
A kid. About Danny's age. He says he was with him. That night.

Everyone goes still. The coffee urn ticks.

BILLIE
(softly)
Let him in.
